#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148289} :activity/id #uuid "31cbade4-f4e0-5331-a47d-83fc78a9b856" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050557} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148289 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148748 :tutorial/content "The second-order Cauchy-Euler equation has the form\n\n$$\nax^2y''+bxy'+cy=f(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,$ and $c$ are constants and $a\\ne0.$ We work on $x>0.$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation ($f(x)=0$) can be solved using a trial solution $y=x^\\lambda,$ where $\\lambda$ is a constant to be found. To illustrate, let's solve the following Cauchy-Euler equation:\n\n$$\nx^2y''+xy'-9y=0\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the trial solution $y = x^\\lambda$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = \\lambda x^{\\lambda -1}, \\qquad y'' = \\lambda(\\lambda-1)x^{\\lambda-2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}+x\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}-9{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }+\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }-9{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }(\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+\\lambda -9) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+\\lambda -9 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-9 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={c}_{1}{x}^{3}+{c}_{2}{x}^{-3} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{x}^{3}+\\frac{{c}_{2}}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "98a1c410-90bd-5aa5-b600-e871f7d98410" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4070 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "716c1708-97de-52e9-8e14-48da9b61f1b6" :step/math-academy-id 24280 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148290}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148290 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147433 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147350} :knowledge-poin